# Dashboard Builder Guide

Style conventions and patterns for building A7S Skipper dashboards. Follow these when creating or modifying dashboard YAML files.

## Design-system source of truth

- Use the shared A7S Skipper design system as the source of truth for styling decisions.
- Prefer canonical chart palette names: `default`, `ocean`, `sunset`.
- Prefer named design-system colors and semantic roles over custom hex values.
- Keep semantic colors meaningful: success/warning/error/info are for status, not arbitrary decoration.
- If an MCP client is available, inspect `skipper://design-system` and `skipper://design-system/palettes` before choosing widget colors.

## RBAC v2 source of truth

- Access is managed through organization-level grants that bind to dashboard resource paths.
- Keep dashboard paths stable and intentional: renaming paths changes the resource names grants match against.
- API tokens are org-scoped bearer credentials resolved through grants.

## Root structure

```text
dashboards/<root>/
  +layout.yaml                    # Root metadata (name, icon, order, default_dashboard)
  _shared/
    _filters.yaml                 # Shared filters (inherited by all dashboards in root)
    _mappings.yaml                # Shared value mappings
    _datasources.yaml             # Shared datasources (usually empty, extend per-dashboard)
  01-dashboard-name/              # Directory-based dashboard
    _tab.yaml                     # Dashboard meta (id, title, icon, description, ai)
    # _dashboard.yaml             # Deprecated alias (validator emits warning)
    01-tab-name.yaml              # Tab file
    02-another-tab.yaml
  02-another-dashboard/
    _tab.yaml
    01-tab.yaml
```

### Two valid patterns only

1. Single flat tab file (everything in one YAML).
2. Directory mode with strict split:
   - `_tab.yaml` meta only
   - `_shared/*.yaml` shared data definitions
   - `NN-*.yaml` tab-content files (`sections: [...]`)

In directory mode, `_tab.yaml` must not include `datasources` or `sections`.

### Naming rules

- Root folders and tab files: `NN-kebab-case` prefix, step of 10 for insertion room
- Dashboard IDs: `snake_case` (e.g., `marketing`, `web5_health`)
- Widget IDs: `kebab-case` (e.g., `daily-costs-table`, `revenue-losses-lang`)
- Datasource keys: `snake_case` (e.g., `daily_marketing`, `by_media_source`)
- Section IDs: `snake_case` (e.g., `overall`, `by_media_source`)

## `+layout.yaml`

```yaml
name: Marketing
icon: HatGlasses
description: Marketing dashboards
order: 1
settings:
  default_dashboard: 01-marketing
```

## `_tab.yaml` — dashboard metadata (`_dashboard.yaml` deprecated + warning)

Every dashboard should have a rich AI block for the assistant:

```yaml
id: marketing
title: Marketing
description: Daily marketing performance — spend, revenue, ROI by source, language, campaign, ad
icon: Megaphone
ai:
  roles:
    preferred: buyer
    extra: [owner, marketing_analyst]
  dataset:
    name: Marketing Performance
    description: >
      Daily marketing data: ad spend, revenue (gross/net, 3-day/full attribution), ROI,
      conversion metrics (actions, trials, clicks, impressions), broken down by media source,
      language, campaign, adset, and ad.
    language: en
    date_column: date
    dimensions: [date, media_source, language, campaign, adset, ad]
    metrics:
      costs: SUM(costs)
      revenue_net: SUM(revenue_net)
      roi_net: (SUM(revenue_net) - SUM(costs)) / NULLIF(SUM(costs), 0) * 100
      ecpa: SUM(costs) / NULLIF(SUM(action), 0)
    business:
      rules:
        - "ROI Net below -45% is highlighted red — critical threshold."
        - "Tab contains drill-down from daily overview → media source → language → campaign → ad."
  welcome: "Ask about marketing spend, revenue, and ROI."
  welcome_ru: "Задайте вопрос о маркетинговых расходах, выручке и ROI."
  examples:
    - "What is the total spend and ROI for the last 7 days?"
    - "Which media source has the best ROI?"
  examples_ru:
    - "Какие общие расходы и ROI за последние 7 дней?"
    - "Какой медиа-источник с лучшим ROI?"
```

### AI metadata checklist

- `roles.preferred` + `roles.extra` — audience roles from root `_context.yaml` (`dashboards/<root>/_context.yaml`)
- `dataset.name` — short name of what data is here
- `dataset.description` — one paragraph explaining all dimensions and metrics available
- `dataset.language` — default AI response language
- `dataset.date_column` — primary time column
- `dataset.dimensions` and `dataset.metrics` — enumerate all
- `dataset.business.rules` — domain-specific thresholds, conventions, gotchas
- `welcome` / `welcome_ru` — greeting for the AI chat
- `examples` / `examples_ru` — 3–4 example questions (English + Russian)

## Tab files

Each tab file = one conceptual view. Minimal structure:

```yaml
title: By media source
datasources:
  by_media_source:
    ai_hint: "Aggregated by media source. One row per source."
    sql: |
      SELECT media_source
           , SUM(costs) AS costs
           , SUM(revenue_net) AS revenue_net
      FROM marts.marketing_creatives
      WHERE ${filters.date_range('toDate(install_date)')}
      GROUP BY media_source
      ORDER BY costs DESC
sections:
  - id: by_media_source
    ai_hint: "Compare performance across ad networks."
    widgets:
      - id: media-source-table
        type: rich_table
        ...
```

### No standalone section files

- Do not create files shaped as top-level `id + widgets`.
- Every tab content file must use top-level `sections: [...]`.
- If you split one tab into multiple files inside a tab directory, each file is still a tab-content fragment and must have `sections`.

### Pattern: drill-down tab hierarchy

Structure tabs from high-level → granular:

1. **Overall** — daily aggregated, no grouping
2. **By dimension** — grouped by one dimension (media source, language)
3. **By multiple** — grouped by 2–3 dimensions (source × language × campaign)
4. **Chart tabs** — loss analysis, cohort, YoY comparison

## SQL conventions

```sql
-- Leading-comma alignment
SELECT toDate(install_date) AS date
     , SUM(costs) AS costs
     , SUM(revenue_net) AS revenue_net
FROM marts.marketing_creatives
WHERE ${filters.date_range('toDate(install_date)')}
GROUP BY date
ORDER BY date DESC
```

- Leading commas for column list (easy to add/remove columns)
- `${filters.<id>('column_expr')}` for ClickHouse-side filtering
- `GROUP BY` by column name, not ordinal
- `ORDER BY` primary metric `DESC` (or date `DESC` for time-series)
- No trailing semicolons
- Alias all expressions (`SUM(costs) AS costs`)

## `ai_hint` everywhere

Add `ai_hint` to datasources, sections, and widgets to help the AI assistant understand context:

```yaml
datasources:
  daily_marketing:
    ai_hint: "Daily aggregated marketing metrics across all sources. Pre-grouped by date."
    sql: ...
sections:
  - id: overall
    ai_hint: "Daily time-series table — one row per date, all key metrics."
    widgets:
      - id: daily-costs-table
        ai_hint: "Main overview table with costs, revenue, ROI, conversion metrics."
```

## Rich table standards

### Column order

Always define `column_order` explicitly — it controls the display order independently of the `columns` array:

```yaml
column_order:
  - date
  - costs
  - revenue_net
  - roi_net
  - action
  - ecpa
```

### Column types pattern

```yaml
columns:
  # Dimension — pinned left, with mapping if needed
  - id: language
    label: Lang
    type: dimension
    width: { type: auto }
    pinned: left
    mapping: { id: languages }

  # Metric — aggregated, formatted
  - id: costs
    label: Costs
    type: metric
    aggregate: sum
    format: { type: currency, decimals: 0 }

  # Calculated — expression with NULLIF guard
  - id: roi_net
    label: ROI Net
    type: calculated
    expression: (SUM(revenue_net) - SUM(costs)) / NULLIF(SUM(costs), 0) * 100
    format: { type: percent, decimals: 1, suffix: "%" }

  # eCPA, CPC, CPM — always guard division by zero
  - id: ecpa
    label: eCPA
    type: calculated
    expression: SUM(costs) / NULLIF(SUM(action), 0)
    format: { type: currency, decimals: 2 }
```

### Visualization defaults

```yaml
visualization:
  zebra: true
  zebra_rows: odd
```

### ROI conditional formatting

Standard red/white/green color scale with -45% threshold:

```yaml
conditional_formatting:
  - column: roi_net
    type: color_scale
    value_sql: |
      CASE
        WHEN costs > 2000 AND roi_net < -45 THEN -100
        WHEN roi_net > 0 THEN 100
        ELSE roi_net
      END
    steps:
      - { value: -45, color: "#F7A2A2" }
      - { value: -44.99, color: "#ffffff" }
      - { value: 0, color: "#ffffff" }
      - { value: 0.01, color: "#91E2AF" }
```

Apply this to all ROI columns (roi_net, roi_3_net, roi_30, roi_90, roi_180, roi_360).

### Sorting and export

Always enable:

```yaml
sorting:
  enabled: true
  default:
    - id: costs
      desc: true
export:
  enabled: true
  formats: [csv, excel]
```

### Column filtering (for drill-down tabs)

Enable on tabs with grouping where users need to search:

```yaml
filtering:
  enabled: true
  show_filter_row: true
  global_search:
    enabled: true
    show_token_pills: true
  columns:
    - { id: language, type: select, multi: true }
    - { id: media_source, type: select, multi: true }
    - { id: campaign, type: text, match: contains }
    - { id: costs, type: number, operator: ">" }
    - { id: roi_net, type: number, operator: ">" }
```

## Chart patterns

### Loss analysis (horizontal bar)

```yaml
- id: revenue-losses-lang
  type: chart
  col_span: 12
  datasource: revenue_losses_by_lang
  preset: loss_analysis
  title: "Revenue Losses by Language"
  series:
    - metric: profit
```

### Cohort (cumulative curves)

```yaml
- id: cohort-cumulative-revenue
  type: chart
  col_span: 12
  datasource: cohort_grow
  preset: cohort
  title: "Cohort grow by days"
  series:
    - metric: cumulative_revenue
      type: line
```

### YoY comparison

```yaml
- id: revenue-net-yoy
  type: chart
  col_span: 12
  datasource: yoy_metrics
  preset: comparison
  drill_down:
    columns: [year, month, day]
    default: month
  title: "Revenue Net YoY"
  series:
    - metric: revenue_net
      type: line
```

## Pivot table pattern

```yaml
- id: payment-pivot-table
  type: rich_table
  title: "Revenue by Payment System"
  col_span: 15
  datasource: payment_pivot
  pivot:
    rows:
      - id: language
        label: Language
    columns:
      - id: data_source
        label: Data source
        type: string
    values:
      - id: revenue_net
        label: Revenue
        aggregate: sum
        format: { type: currency, decimals: 0 }
    totals:
      row: true
      column: true
    grand_total_label: Grand Total
  columns:
    - id: language
      label: Language
      type: dimension
      pinned: left
      width: { type: fixed, value: 120 }
    - id: grand_total
      label: Grand Total
      type: metric
      aggregate: sum
      format: { type: currency, decimals: 0 }
```

## View datasources (DuckDB JOINs)

When combining two base ClickHouse sources:

```yaml
overall_daily:
  ai_hint: "Combined daily view: marketing metrics joined with payment source revenue."
  type: view
  sql: |
    SELECT t.date, t.costs, t.revenue_net,
      d.Appstore, d.Gplay, d.Stripe
    FROM {{source:daily_marketing}} t
    LEFT JOIN {{source:daily_ds_revenue}} d ON t.date = d.date
    ORDER BY t.date DESC
```

## Shared filters

Standard date range filter (root-level `_shared/_filters.yaml`):

```yaml
- id: date_range
  type: date_range
  column: install_date
  presets:
    - { id: today, label: Today, tag: Live }
    - { id: yesterday, label: Yesterday, tag: Live }
    - { id: last_7_days, label: "Last 7 days", tag: Rolling }
    - { id: last_14_days, label: "Last 14 days", tag: Rolling }
    - { id: last_30_days, label: "Last 30 days", tag: Rolling }
    - { id: this_month, label: This month }
    - { id: last_month, label: Last month }
    - { id: last_6_months, label: "Last 6 months" }
    - { id: last_12_months, label: "Last 12 months" }
  default:
    preset: last_14_days
```

## Checklist for new dashboards

1. Create dashboard directory `NN-name/` inside the target root
2. Write `_tab.yaml` with full AI metadata block (`_dashboard.yaml` is deprecated and triggers warning)
3. Create tab files `01-overview.yaml`, `02-detail.yaml`, etc.
4. Each tab-content file: `title`, `datasources` (with `ai_hint`), `sections` (with `ai_hint`)
5. Rich tables: `column_order`, full column definitions, zebra, conditional formatting, sorting, export
6. Charts: use appropriate preset (trend, loss_analysis, cohort, comparison)
7. Add `ai_hint` to all datasources, sections, and widgets
8. Run `a7s-skipper validate` to check schema compliance

## MCP data exploration tools

The `preview_query` and `explore_metric` MCP tools return query results through three delivery channels:

| Channel | Rows | Format | Purpose |
|---------|------|--------|---------|
| `content[0].text` | ≤ 500 | JSON | LLM context (always present) |
| `structuredContent` | ≤ 1,000 | JSON table | UI clients (Claude Desktop, Claude.ai) — zero tokens |
| `resource_link` | ≤ 10,000 | CSV | Full data on demand via `skipper://query-results/{id}` |

- Results with ≤ 500 rows are returned fully inline (no `resource_link`).
- Results with > 500 rows are truncated in `content.text` with a `resource_link` to the full dataset.
- The `resource_link` uses **lazy evaluation**: only query parameters are stored at tool-call time; the full query executes on the first `resources/read` request.
- Cache TTL: 1 hour.
- The `structuredContent` table always includes `sql` and `execution_time_ms` metadata.
